Subject: [PATCH] renku_webdavd: the boot volume as a WebDAV share for the Mac host

run-qemu-renku-arm64.sh (and so MacRenkuOS's `renkuos`) mounts the guest's
/boot on the Mac, where Finder lists it under Locations like any network
volume. QEMU's cocoa display has no shared-folder channel and Haiku has no
virtio-9p/virtiofs driver, and the one network file protocol macOS mounts
read-write with nothing to install, no root and no dialog is WebDAV
(mount_webdav -S) -- so the guest serves one.

renku_webdavd is a small threaded HTTP/1.1 server (POSIX + libnetwork
only) implementing what macOS's webdavfs needs to mount read-write:
OPTIONS, PROPFIND (depth 0/1), GET/HEAD with single ranges, PUT (including
chunked bodies), MKCOL, DELETE, COPY, MOVE, PROPPATCH (accepted, not
stored) and LOCK/UNLOCK -- webdavfs mounts read-only unless the server
advertises DAV class 2; locks are granted, not enforced.

- It listens on port 2280 and only accepts connections from 10.0.2.2
  (where QEMU usermode networking delivers a hostfwd'ed connection) and
  127.0.0.1. With --bridged the guest is also on the real LAN, which would
  otherwise get read-write access to the whole volume.
- The share is /RenkuOS/ -> /boot; the last path component is what macOS
  names the volume.
- macOS writes AppleDouble "._name" files (extended attributes, including
  the com.apple.provenance/quarantine nearly every file carries) and
  .DS_Store next to everything it copies. Those are held in memory instead
  of being written into the guest's folders.
- macOS sends file names decomposed (Hangul NFD); they are stored composed
  (NFC), the form Haiku itself and its input method produce, so a folder
  named in Korean from the Mac shows correctly in Tracker.

Started by a launch_daemon service in data/launch/system, through
`sh -c "exec ..."` for the same reason sshd is (0016).

